Components & Roles:

AWS Cognito: Handles authentication, password hashing, MFA, and issuance of JWT Access Tokens.

DynamoDB: Stores multi-tenant metadata—Customers, Sites, User Grants (e.g., "User A can read Site 101, but not Site 102"), and Audit Logs.

AWS Gateway (Indus.CloudGateway): An ASP.NET Core application hosted on EC2/ECS. It validates user JWTs against DynamoDB permissions and proxies authorized requests through the correct site tunnel.

Why:

Decoupling: Mobile clients do not need to know where plants are or how to connect to them. They talk to one gateway.

Centralized Revocation: If an operator is terminated, disabling them in DynamoDB immediately blocks access across all 50 plants, even if their site tunnel credentials remain active.

D. User Interface (React Mobile PWA)
What we are doing:
Building a responsive React web application optimized for mobile screen sizes, hosted cheaply on AWS S3 and served globally via CloudFront CDN.

Why:

PWA vs Native: Avoids Apple App Store / Google Play Store approval cycles and cross-platform native maintenance. It can be saved to a mobile home screen like an app.

Zero Secrets in Frontend: The React app contains zero Cloudflare keys, zero database credentials, and zero internal plant URLs. It only holds the user's short-lived JWT token.

3. Data Flow Deep Dives
Deep Dive 1: Real-Time Live Tag Streaming
How a phone displays a live updating motor speed (e.g., 99.98 %) without crushing plant hardware.

[Mobile Browser]          [AWS Gateway]           [Site 101 API]        [Local SCADA]
       │                        │                        │                    │
       ├─ WebSocket Subscribe ─►│                        │                    │
       │  (Site 101, Tags)      ├─ Check DynamoDB Grant  │                    │
       │                        │  (User -> Site 101?)   │                    │
       │                        │ ── Authenticated ──►   │                    │
       │                        ├─ Open Shared Tunnel ──►│                    │
       │                        │  Connection            ├─ Read Live Cache ─►│
       │                        │                        │◄─ Current Values ──┘
       │                        │◄── Batch Delta Stream ─┤
       │◄── SignalR Fan-Out ────┤    (Every 250-1000ms)  │
Multiplexing / Fan-out: If 10 engineers are viewing Site 101 on their phones, the AWS Gateway opens one single connection to Site 101's plant PC. It then duplicates (fans out) those updates to all 10 phones, preventing 10 separate connections from overwhelming local plant memory.

Coalescing: High-frequency tags are batched into 250ms–1000ms intervals. If a temperature sensor updates 50 times a second, the gateway sends only the latest value once per interval to conserve mobile data.

Deep Dive 2: Historical Trend Querying
How an engineer views a 24-hour graph of temperature data.

[Mobile App]              [AWS Gateway]            [Site 101 API]      [PostgreSQL DB]
     │                          │                        │                    │
     ├── GET /sites/101/trends ─►│                        │                    │
     │   ?range=24h&tag=TEMP1   ├─ Verify Authorization  │                    │
     │                          ├─ Proxy over Tunnel ───►│                    │
     │                          │  (With Service Token)  ├── Downsampled SQL ─►│
     │                          │                        │   Query            │
     │                          │                        │◄─ 500 Data Points ─┘
     │◄── JSON Chart Data ──────┼◄── Return Stream ──────┘
Downsampling at the Source: Querying 24 hours of raw 1-second data yields 86,400 rows. Sending 86,400 raw points to a phone will crash the browser UI. The local Site API downsamples the data in SQL (e.g., returning min/max/average over 5-minute buckets) so only ~300–500 points travel across the network.

Strict Time Bounds: Every query must enforce UTC start/end times and maximum sample limits to prevent memory exhaustion attacks.

4. End-to-End Security Model
       MOBILE APP                       AWS CLOUD EDGE                     PLANT NETWORK
  ┌───────────────────┐             ┌────────────────────┐            ┌────────────────────┐
  │  Bearer JWT Token │ ──────────► │ Token Verification │            │  Outbound-Only     │
  │  (Identity & Role)│             │ Authorization RBAC │            │  TLS Tunnel        │
  └───────────────────┘             └─────────┬──────────┘            └─────────▲──────────┘
                                              │                                 │
                                              │ Service Token                   │
                                              └─────────────────────────────────┘
                                              Cloudflare Access Header Validation
Authentication (Who are you?): Managed by AWS Cognito. Users enter credentials and complete MFA. Cognito issues a short-lived OAuth2 Access Token.

Authorization (What can you see?): Managed by AWS Gateway + DynamoDB. Every request checks: Does User X have an active grant for Site Y? If no, access is denied.

Machine Authentication (Is this caller allowed to hit the plant?): Managed by Cloudflare Access. The plant's tunnel rejects any request missing the valid CF-Access-Client-Id header secret generated by Cloudflare and stored in AWS Secrets Manager.

